#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133621 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf385144-2f48-5874-8c0d-dec31d8fc6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "69c1029c-777f-5a16-8ff5-9f8f0c9a48b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9071" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,0)$ and $(0,2)$, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. According to Green's theorem,\n$\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\cos{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y}= \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d A$,\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2116/Source/Images/db911dc99f33b91c66fb04d8b1e4301f.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= \\cos{y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= x\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} =-\\sin y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = \\sin y\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)\\cos ydx + x\\sin ydy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\sin y - (-\\sin y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2\\sin ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133622 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "2109910f-fc87-5e56-b2f6-12132b9f6073" :question/math-academy-id "e-9072" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$, where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0)$, $(1,2)$ and $(0,2)$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2116/Source/Images/bdec15081a2e1ef58dcb3545c680a71d.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= 4x^2y\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= 2y^2\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = 4x^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 1,\\, 2x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)4x^{2}ydx + 2y^{2}dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(0 - 4x^{2})dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{2x}^{2}(-4x^{2})dydx \\\\\n&=-4∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}[y]_{2x}^{2}dx \\\\\n&=-4∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}(2 - 2x)dx \\\\\n&=-8∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}(1 - x)dx \\\\\n&=-8∫_{0}^{1}x^{2} - x^{3}dx \\\\\n&=-8[\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{x^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-8(\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&=-8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133623 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48652211-e469-55b9-96cb-2526a4135d34" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Repeated Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "3ff94d55-a99a-5de6-bc84-410d03f18a28" :question/math-academy-id "e-9086" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1)$, traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2116/Source/Images/d31d071642487b14587e48bac2c69b6a.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= x\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= x^2\\cos{y}-y^2\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = x\\cos y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 2x\\cos y\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): -y\\leq x\\leq y,\\, 0\\leq y \\leq 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)x\\sin ydx + (x^{2}\\cos y - y^{2})dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(2x\\cos y - x\\cos y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x\\cos ydA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{-y}^{y}x\\cos ydxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48652211-e469-55b9-96cb-2526a4135d34" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Repeated Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3155b21-8184-5f26-aefe-94b0b551a2a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2x}x^{2}ydydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130504} {:answer/id #uuid "b32f28b7-061a-52d0-8909-5d75b51a5a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{x}2xdydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130505} {:answer/id #uuid "f7749096-f729-51e6-bd78-edb4fc70a39a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2x}2xydydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130506} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6df373-87ac-5147-ae35-aba004aecd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2x}xy^{2}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130507} {:answer/id #uuid "359bbcda-7289-59c3-9c9f-4987e5a75c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{2x}x^{2}y^{2}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7749096-f729-51e6-bd78-edb4fc70a39a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2x}2xydydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130506} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3cce8ac-995a-5b46-bbaa-693995f7e957" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130503}] :question/id #uuid "700fbfa1-78ed-5b39-a539-ef3e4dd8874d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108467" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0, 0), (1, 0)$ and $(1, 2)$, traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. Express $∮_(C)xy^{2}dx + 2x^{2}ydy$ as a repeated integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf385144-2f48-5874-8c0d-dec31d8fc6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0001f248-97ab-52c6-9e5c-7c6078a09765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(y^{3} + x^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130510} {:answer/id #uuid "029545d7-cd8c-5469-88dd-26f8a9109a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x - y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130511} {:answer/id #uuid "345d7221-f721-54c5-a555-6bdcff4a55ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x + y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130512} {:answer/id #uuid "372fa338-ef4b-5a6c-b9bf-6f0bab6280c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(x^{3} + y^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130513} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8de9a9-8505-5d69-af42-bffaf05e4f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(y - x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "029545d7-cd8c-5469-88dd-26f8a9109a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x - y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130511} :answer-field/id #uuid "140f1d47-7624-5cf3-856c-edeb667a384c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130509}] :question/id #uuid "00a6ca58-bb1e-5472-9379-ee67967bcfed" :question/math-academy-id "q-108588" :question/problem "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)$ and $(1, 1)$, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. According to Green's theorem,\n\n$∮_(C)y^{2}dx + x^{2}dy = ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x, y)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad57ff4a-99a2-5311-aafe-746a5596ab6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130540} {:answer/id #uuid "c157781c-d437-5f44-8194-228b83ac3f58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130541} {:answer/id #uuid "d674f3e0-1362-58f6-8915-972276b5d7f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130542} {:answer/id #uuid "321658aa-2b8c-52dd-b230-48356bc1686b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130543} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8cfb24-004d-51dc-9d73-20ed9f2a1a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d674f3e0-1362-58f6-8915-972276b5d7f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130542} :answer-field/id #uuid "66d81cc1-bd2c-5254-b5d7-8369fbbd42eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130539}] :question/id #uuid "4e3c164e-33ad-5643-8072-cdedeec68994" :question/math-academy-id "q-109147" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $∮_(C)(e^{x} - 2x^{2}y)dx + (e^{y} + 2xy^{2})dy$, where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0, 0)$, $(2, 2)$ and $(-2, 2)$, traversed counterclockwise."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf385144-2f48-5874-8c0d-dec31d8fc6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3876569-70ff-5806-b162-f30782d82093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{x}\\sin 2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130546} {:answer/id #uuid "e4985457-277e-5361-967f-168e1610fb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}\\cos 2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130547} {:answer/id #uuid "18c1bbcb-496e-5a0b-b20b-e2235a9f1188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{x}(\\sin 2y - \\cos 2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130548} {:answer/id #uuid "689e3104-9abd-5d6f-9fd7-4e477ef86017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}(\\sin 2y - 2\\cos 2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130549} {:answer/id #uuid "765c7734-b45e-5f59-9bc3-bc3840a75c7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}(2\\sin 2y + \\cos 2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3876569-70ff-5806-b162-f30782d82093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{x}\\sin 2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130546} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e0f172e-102f-5816-b334-094da7961722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130545}] :question/id #uuid "4b395a92-9fb2-5e47-8972-f8a867f5011a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109161" :question/problem "Consider the path $C$, in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. According to Green's theorem,\n\n$∮_(C)e^{x}\\cos 2ydx + e^{x}\\sin 2ydy = ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x, y)$."}]}
